prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages patent applications, office actions, filings, and examination work on a prosecution docket.

The people involved are patent attorneys, docket clerks, examiners, and inventors. One person may hold both the patent attorney and docket clerk roles, and one person may hold both the examiner and inventor roles.

The resources are patent applications, office actions, and filings. Every patent application records its inventor, prosecuting attorney, assigned examiner, team, confidentiality, bar date, and status.

Each requester carries their clearance.

Each request carries the current date, whether it is made on the firm network, whether there is a court order, and whether MFA was used.

An examiner may examine a patent application only when MFA was used.

An examiner who has not used MFA must not examine a patent application.

An examiner who is a named inventor on a patent application must recuse and may not examine that application.

An examiner who is not a named inventor on the application and has used MFA may examine it.

A request by an examiner to examine an application that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Role enum ["patentAttorney", "docketClerk", "examiner", "inventor"];

entity Person {
    roles: Set<Role>,
    clearance: String,
};

entity Team;

entity PatentApplication {
    inventor: Person,
    prosecutingAttorney: Person,
    assignedExaminer: Person,
    team: Team,
    confidentiality: String,
    barDate: datetime,
    status: String,
};

entity OfficeAction;

entity Filing;

action examinePatentApplication appliesTo {
    principal: [Person],
    resource: [PatentApplication],
    context: {
        currentDate: datetime,
        onFirmNetwork: Bool,
        hasCourtOrder: Bool,
        mfaUsed: Bool,
    },
};
"
}
